From f69db1d28dad4028d260961e1d65099c0fc945e8 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Nieyin345 <nieyin0703@outlook.com>
Date: Sat, 24 Oct 2026 22:59:55 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] feat(markdown): reshell the chrome in Mcode's design tokens
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Same mechanism and the same 54 chrome token values as the Docs reshell
(05da63c) and the HTML reshell that precedes this commit, so the three shells
read as one product. The gate is the UA product token (/\bMcode\//), not
window.desktop: Markdown's preload exposes window.markdownApi and never
`desktop`.

Markdown has one hazard Docs and HTML do not: its preview paper, .doc-page
(App.tsx:1262, where the body itself renders), paints with var(--surface) —
the very token that has to change for the chrome. Re-pointing :root alone
would tint the document. The file therefore pins the paper and ink tokens back
to their tokens.css values inside .doc-page (light, dark and system-dark), and
the suite asserts both that the paper is identical in hosted and standalone
runs and that every pinned value still equals tokens.css — so an upstream paper
colour change turns the suite red with a message naming the token, rather than
silently moving the document.

The paper themes in markdown-themes.css are unaffected: their selectors carry
an html[data-markdown-theme='…'] prefix (specificity 0,2,1) and beat the
.doc-page pin (0,1,0) regardless of order, so a theme the user picked still
wins for the paper.

Scope is otherwise narrow:

- Editor kernel, ProseMirror schema, source view, export and print are
  untouched.
- --md-* (link, code, quote, table header, diagram, page shadow), --mdt-*
  (paper themes) and --redact-* stay the app's own.
- Per-app brand accents (--accent, --word-blue) are not overridden.
